1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,899
On the morning of August twenty-second, 1986, a man named Ephriam Che walked

2
00:00:06,899 --> 00:00:10,664
down to the lake below his home in northwest Cameroon.

3
00:00:11,449 --> 00:00:15,825
The water, normally clear blue, had turned a dull red.

4
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:19,309
The waterfall at the lake's outlet was dry.

5
00:00:19,309 --> 00:00:21,173
And there was no sound.

6
00:00:21,173 --> 00:00:21,902
No birds.

7
00:00:21,902 --> 00:00:22,793
No insects.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,504
In the valleys around the lake, at least seventeen hundred people were dead.

9
00:00:28,504 --> 00:00:30,150
None of them had drowned.

10
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:32,849
The lake had killed them through the air.

11
00:00:34,037 --> 00:00:39,334
Lake Nyos sits high in the grasslands, in a volcanic crater only a few hundred

12
00:00:39,334 --> 00:00:40,013
years old.

13
00:00:40,013 --> 00:00:45,106
It's more than two hundred metres deep, and the farmland around it is rich.

14
00:00:45,889 --> 00:00:50,459
At about half past nine on the night of August twenty-first, people near

15
00:00:50,459 --> 00:00:52,110
the lake heard a rumbling.

16
00:00:52,110 --> 00:00:54,902
It lasted perhaps fifteen or twenty seconds.

17
00:00:55,566 --> 00:00:58,839
Ephriam Che thought it sounded like a rockslide.

18
00:00:58,839 --> 00:01:03,885
He saw a white mist rise from the water, assumed rain was coming, and went

19
00:01:03,885 --> 00:01:05,181
to bed feeling ill.

20
00:01:05,965 --> 00:01:10,681
Halima Suley, who lived nearby, heard what sounded like "the shouting of

21
00:01:10,681 --> 00:01:11,532
many voices."

22
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:16,380
Then a strong wind swept through her family's compound, and she collapsed,

23
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:17,755
"like a dead person."

24
00:01:18,555 --> 00:01:20,970
When she woke, her children were dead.

25
00:01:20,970 --> 00:01:25,609
So were thirty-one other members of her family, and four hundred of their

26
00:01:25,609 --> 00:01:26,054
cattle.

27
00:01:26,825 --> 00:01:31,799
Survivors woke six to thirty-six hours later, weak and confused.

28
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:34,909
In Nyos village, most people never woke.

29
00:01:34,909 --> 00:01:38,407
That morning, Che found Suley among her dead.

30
00:01:38,407 --> 00:01:42,915
"On that day," he said, "there were no flies on the dead."

31
00:01:42,915 --> 00:01:44,703
The flies had died too.

32
00:01:46,415 --> 00:01:48,297
Scientists arrived within days.

33
00:01:48,297 --> 00:01:50,482
Their first suspect was the volcano.

34
00:01:51,085 --> 00:01:55,597
But there was no lava, no fresh ash, and no reported earthquake.

35
00:01:55,597 --> 00:01:57,078
The water wasn't hot.

36
00:01:57,078 --> 00:02:00,886
And the lake floor showed no sign of being blown open.

37
00:02:01,493 --> 00:02:05,811
Many survivors had skin sores and believed the gas had burned them.

38
00:02:05,811 --> 00:02:10,709
Doctors concluded most of those wounds came from lying motionless for hours.

39
00:02:10,709 --> 00:02:14,189
Tests found no evidence of cyanide or carbon monoxide.

40
00:02:14,883 --> 00:02:16,651
There had been one warning.

41
00:02:16,651 --> 00:02:21,760
In 1984, thirty-seven people died on a low stretch of road beside Lake Monoun,

42
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,380
about a hundred kilometres to the south.

43
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:26,672
No eruption was found there either.

44
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:29,800
The lake held the rest of the answer.

45
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:32,808
Its surface was covered in floating vegetation.

46
00:02:32,808 --> 00:02:37,543
A surge had washed twenty-five metres up the southern shore, and water had

47
00:02:37,543 --> 00:02:40,743
been thrown over a rocky point eighty metres high.

48
00:02:41,475 --> 00:02:45,692
Water pulled up from the depths fizzed as it reached the surface.

49
00:02:45,692 --> 00:02:47,898
It was loaded with carbon dioxide.

50
00:02:48,611 --> 00:02:51,911
The gas hadn't come from an eruption that night.

51
00:02:51,911 --> 00:02:53,904
The lake had been holding it.

52
00:02:53,904 --> 00:02:56,516
And on August twenty-first, it let go.

53
00:02:58,199 --> 00:03:02,167
About eighty kilometres beneath the region, there is magma.

54
00:03:02,167 --> 00:03:07,682
Carbon dioxide from it rises slowly through the crust, dissolves into groundwater,

55
00:03:07,682 --> 00:03:10,305
and seeps into the bottom of Lake Nyos.

56
00:03:11,069 --> 00:03:14,711
In most lakes, that gas escapes as the water mixes.

57
00:03:14,711 --> 00:03:17,996
But Lake Nyos is deep, sheltered, and layered.

58
00:03:17,996 --> 00:03:23,281
The bottom water, heavy with dissolved gas, is denser than the water above

59
00:03:23,281 --> 00:03:24,781
it, so it stays down.

60
00:03:25,559 --> 00:03:30,325
Two hundred metres of water press on it with about twenty times the force

61
00:03:30,325 --> 00:03:31,500
of the atmosphere.

62
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,549
Under that pressure, water can hold an enormous amount of gas.

63
00:03:36,328 --> 00:03:39,930
It's like a sealed bottle of sparkling water.

64
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:41,450
Cap on, no bubbles.

65
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:43,931
Open it, and the gas comes out.

66
00:03:43,931 --> 00:03:48,012
At Nyos, the cap was the weight of the lake itself.

67
00:03:48,698 --> 00:03:52,642
Now imagine some of that deep water is pushed upward.

68
00:03:52,642 --> 00:03:56,289
As it rises, the pressure falls and bubbles form.

69
00:03:56,289 --> 00:04:01,647
The bubbles make the water lighter, so it rises faster and releases more

70
00:04:01,647 --> 00:04:01,944
gas.

71
00:04:01,944 --> 00:04:04,326
Once it starts, it feeds itself.

72
00:04:05,008 --> 00:04:08,660
That runaway release explains the rumbling and the surge.

73
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:13,337
The lake dropped by about a metre, releasing an estimated cubic kilometre

74
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:14,491
of carbon dioxide.

75
00:04:14,491 --> 00:04:18,335
That's roughly four hundred thousand Olympic swimming pools.

76
00:04:19,398 --> 00:04:21,375
It explains the colour, too.

77
00:04:21,375 --> 00:04:23,634
The deep water was rich in iron.

78
00:04:23,634 --> 00:04:28,859
When it reached the air, the iron oxidised, and the lake turned rusty red.

79
00:04:29,558 --> 00:04:32,021
What set it off is still unresolved.

80
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:37,082
A landslide, strong wind, or cool rainwater sinking from the surface could

81
00:04:37,082 --> 00:04:39,065
each have tipped the balance.

82
00:04:39,065 --> 00:04:43,374
A few researchers argue for a pulse of volcanic gas from below.

83
00:04:43,374 --> 00:04:45,494
The mechanism is widely agreed.

84
00:04:45,494 --> 00:04:46,999
The first push is not.

85
00:04:48,068 --> 00:04:50,934
We breathe out carbon dioxide all the time.

86
00:04:50,934 --> 00:04:55,400
It normally makes up about zero point zero four percent of the air.

87
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,266
The danger is concentration.

88
00:04:57,866 --> 00:05:02,803
Carbon dioxide is about one and a half times heavier than air, so the cloud

89
00:05:02,803 --> 00:05:03,988
didn't drift away.

90
00:05:03,988 --> 00:05:08,135
It spilled over the crater rim and flowed downhill, like water.

91
00:05:08,135 --> 00:05:09,978
North, through Nyos village.

92
00:05:09,978 --> 00:05:11,755
West, along the Cha valley.

93
00:05:11,755 --> 00:05:15,573
It stayed lethal up to about ten kilometres from the lake.

94
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:20,946
At around ten percent carbon dioxide, a person can lose consciousness within

95
00:05:20,946 --> 00:05:21,432
minutes.

96
00:05:21,432 --> 00:05:25,140
Above about seventeen percent, it can happen within a minute.

97
00:05:25,140 --> 00:05:29,274
The cloud arrived at night, when most people were indoors or asleep.

98
00:05:30,336 --> 00:05:31,300
And it lingered.

99
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:35,759
For days afterward, people collapsed at low stream crossings where gas was

100
00:05:35,759 --> 00:05:36,603
still pooling.

101
00:05:37,263 --> 00:05:41,070
An eruption announces itself with heat, ash and fire.

102
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:44,949
At Nyos, there was a rumble, a mist, and then silence.

103
00:05:46,463 --> 00:05:47,836
The gas kept coming.

104
00:05:47,836 --> 00:05:52,159
Within a few years, it was building up again in the deep water.

105
00:05:52,159 --> 00:05:57,032
By the late nineteen-nineties, researchers warned the lake was starting

106
00:05:57,032 --> 00:05:59,434
to look the way it had before 1986.

107
00:06:00,213 --> 00:06:02,363
The fix used the same physics.

108
00:06:02,363 --> 00:06:07,595
Run a pipe from the deep water to the surface, and pump a little water up

109
00:06:07,595 --> 00:06:08,455
to start it.

110
00:06:08,455 --> 00:06:13,901
As it rises, gas comes out, the water gets lighter and rises faster, pulling

111
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:15,980
more deep water in behind it.

112
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:17,700
Then it runs on its own.

113
00:06:18,443 --> 00:06:23,841
In 2001, a French-led team installed the first permanent pipe, drawing water

114
00:06:23,841 --> 00:06:26,326
from about two hundred metres down.

115
00:06:26,326 --> 00:06:31,866
The chain reaction that killed in 1986 was now running through a pipe, slowly,

116
00:06:31,866 --> 00:06:33,144
and under control.

117
00:06:33,983 --> 00:06:38,915
One pipe would take decades to clear the lake, so in 2011, two more were

118
00:06:38,915 --> 00:06:39,326
added.

119
00:06:39,326 --> 00:06:42,477
Scientists have monitored the lake since 1986.

120
00:06:42,477 --> 00:06:47,477
And in 2020, members of the French team reported that a single pipe could

121
00:06:47,477 --> 00:06:50,834
now keep pace with the carbon dioxide seeping in.

122
00:06:51,871 --> 00:06:56,505
Before Monoun and Nyos, there was no scientific record of a lake doing this.

123
00:06:56,505 --> 00:06:58,761
Now it has a name: a limnic eruption.

124
00:06:58,761 --> 00:07:03,213
Researchers are watching other lakes, including Lake Kivu, between Rwanda

125
00:07:03,213 --> 00:07:07,664
and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, where around two million people

126
00:07:07,664 --> 00:07:08,884
live near the shore.

127
00:07:10,111 --> 00:07:13,354
Ephriam Che found Lake Nyos red and silent.

128
00:07:13,354 --> 00:07:18,333
Now, a pipe sends a column of water and gas into the air above it.

129
00:07:19,761 --> 00:07:24,904
The carbon dioxide is still seeping up from below, exactly as it did before

130
00:07:24,904 --> 00:07:25,247
1986.

131
00:07:25,247 --> 00:07:29,498
The difference is that now, it doesn't get the chance to wait.
